Z||000000||JohnnyK||09-13-2000||06:21 PM||||Well, I had a theory. Put some washers in between the slave cylinder plunger, and the black "lever" thing that moves on the transmission, for that extra few millimeters of travel. Well, it didn't help at all. So I assume the problem is a clutch fork or throwout bearing? (My problem, shifts fine when car is off, but when car is on, can't even get it in gear). Tommorow, at my school, I'm gonna drop the cradle and tear it apart (wish I could do it at my house so that I could work more than 1 hour a day, but I don't have a garage to work in) If anyone has a suggestion that I haven't tried yet, tell me now before I destroy the tranny..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||JohnnyK||09-13-2000||09:52 PM||||I'm going to try this tommorow before anything major. Push the clutch as far as it will go, and then push hard on the lever on the transmission, and have someone try to change gears. I think this would give me an accurate test? If it shifts, it is the hydraulic system... if not, I dig in..||||reg||1
Z||000002||Patrick||09-14-2000||02:22 AM||||<BR>If you are getting just under an inch (about 15/16) of travel at the slave, then the clutch should disengage and the tranny should shift. If you don't have that much travel at the slave, then you may be dropping the cradle for nothing.<P>I'm surprised that you had that "theory" about the washers. I'm surprised because if you had read earlier clutch threads posted here, you'd have discovered that we've shot down similar ideas to that many, many times.||||reg||4
Z||000003||JohnnyK||09-14-2000||08:46 AM||||Well sorry... Geez..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Well, the actual slave rod moves more than an inch, by far. I'm not sure what the lever travels, but it looks like more than an inch too.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||09-14-2000||04:05 PM||||Nope, I measured and it is about exactly one inch. A tiny bit less... Why are you so pissy, Patty?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Steven||09-14-2000||04:29 PM||||Before you drop the engine and trans. Take a moment to look at the clutch fork lever. Is your lever the stamped steel piece or the cast steel. The reason I ask is that I know first hand that the stamp steel piece is known to break. That is why GM discon'd it, and replaced it with that cast part. The stamped part over time breaks on the back side where it clamps on to the clutch fork shaft. When this happens, the lever moves, but the clutch fork shaft does not. My advice would be to use a paint pin, white out, something to draw a line thur the fork shaft. Have someone push the pedal, and make sure the shaft is moving, and not just the lever sliping on the shaft.<p>[This message has been edited by Steven (edited 09-14-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000006||JohnnyK||09-14-2000||06:23 PM||||Should have answered this yesterday.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Today, all the fuel lines are disconnected, the coolant drained, the shifter cables disconnected, air filter assembly removes, etc... Still have to do the ecm, and then she's comin out! What else do I have to remove. I can't really thing of anything. The exhaust comes out on the cradle, so I assume thats it...||||reg||1
Z||000007||DJRice||09-14-2000||09:52 PM||||Well, did you get the vacuum lines in the area under the dogbone?  Also, If you have cruise control, then there is one cable to the throttle body you will need to undo as well.  There are also the ground straps that run from the decklid hinges to the block.  There should be one on each side.  <P>As for Patricks "pissy" comment, I will have to agree with him.  There is a post called "Clutch Info Uncovered"  that has probably close to 150 posts by now in which the entire clutch system has been discussed in great depth.   I highly recommend that you read this thread.  It is a very valuable source of experience as far as clutch troubleshooting goes.  I know it helped me alot.  <P>Patrick has always been a very strong proponent of the Forum's "Search" Function.  <BR><P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000008||JohnnyK||09-14-2000||11:39 PM||||Man, I was just kidding..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I checked most threads, but I must have missed that one. Hmm, well, my shop teacher (a mechanic) is pretty sure it is the throw-out bearing or the clutch fork. Lets hope I can have it out by tommorow or monday, and I'll see what actually was wrong. I swear it wasn't the hydraulics..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||Patrick||09-15-2000||04:54 AM||||<BR><B>Dillon</B>, thanks for mentioning the "Clutch info uncovered" thread. I wouldn't have believed that there was anyone here with a clutch problem who hadn't seen this long informative thread. Apparently I was mistaken. Heck, it was just added to only a few days ago on 9-6-00. To make it real easy for anyone to find, here it is:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/000034.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/000034.html</A> <P><B>Johnny</B>, maybe the reason why I'm so "pissy" is because this is the 17th, yes the <B>17th</B> thread you've started about your clutch problems. I can't help but suspect that you haven't made much use of the <I>Search</I> function and read earlier posts where we've discussed at length most (if not all) of the problems you've encountered. This is my <B>15th</B> response to your pleas for help. I've tried to give you a hand and you turn around and ask me why I'm being "pissy"? Maybe you're just kidding around, but I don't see a whole lot of humour in it. If you take a look at the thread mentioned above, you'll get an idea of how frustrated I was with MY clutch problems. I've tried to help you out because of all the trouble I had, so please don't acknowledge my efforts by suggesting that I'm being "pissy".||||reg||1
